WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.356 --> 00:00:01.620
- I want to be bigger than Adele.

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:02.528
- That should be your goal.

00:00:02.528 --> 00:00:04.199
- Yeah the word "Adele" is
just something that no one

00:00:04.199 --> 00:00:05.429
should (beep) with basically.

00:00:05.429 --> 00:00:06.381
What's the worse that can happen?

00:00:06.381 --> 00:00:07.647
The worse that can happen is you fail.

00:00:07.647 --> 00:00:10.232
I've only written songs from the heart.

00:00:10.232 --> 00:00:12.158
I want to keep having
stuff to do everyday.

00:00:12.158 --> 00:00:16.176
♫ Cause darling your soul

00:00:16.176 --> 00:00:20.393
♫ could never grow old it's evergreen.

00:00:20.393 --> 00:00:21.974
(screams)

00:00:21.974 --> 00:00:23.054
- What's up Belief Nation it's Evan.

00:00:23.054 --> 00:00:26.303
My one word is Believe and
I believe that you have the

00:00:26.303 --> 00:00:29.418
ability to do something special
that can change the planet.

00:00:29.418 --> 00:00:30.869
So to help you on your journey today,

00:00:30.869 --> 00:00:33.450
we're going to learn from
singer/songwriter Ed Sheeran

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.161
and my take on his top ten
rules to success, volume two.

00:00:37.161 --> 00:00:38.832
Rule number one is my personal favorite,

00:00:38.832 --> 00:00:43.198
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:43.198 --> 00:00:45.781
(upbeat music)

00:00:54.399 --> 00:00:57.923
♫ I'm in love with your body

00:00:57.923 --> 00:01:00.649
- Now I read that your goal
is to sell 20 million copies

00:01:00.649 --> 00:01:03.974
of this album Divide, and
you want to overtake Adele

00:01:03.974 --> 00:01:05.506
in terms of record sales.

00:01:05.506 --> 00:01:09.442
- Yeah and I feel like me
saying this, a lot of people

00:01:09.442 --> 00:01:12.122
will like, that's such
an arrogant thing to say.

00:01:12.122 --> 00:01:15.707
But I think you need to
choose someone that's at the

00:01:15.707 --> 00:01:16.540
top of their game.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:17.680
Like I probably won't sell
20 million records and I

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:19.430
probably won't ever be as big as Adele,

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:21.391
but if you don't aim for
the top of the mountain

00:01:21.391 --> 00:01:23.277
how are you ever going to get halfway?

00:01:23.277 --> 00:01:24.910
- Right, and set high goals for yourself.

00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:27.572
- Yeah. But the problem
is that my point never

00:01:27.572 --> 00:01:29.389
comes across with that
because that quote is now

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:31.312
just, I want to be bigger than Adele.

00:01:31.312 --> 00:01:32.145
- That should be your goal.

00:01:32.145 --> 00:01:34.101
- Yeah, the word Adele is just
something that no one should

00:01:34.101 --> 00:01:34.934
(bleep) with basically.

00:01:34.934 --> 00:01:37.028
(laughs)

00:01:37.028 --> 00:01:38.409
So if you put that into
any sentence prepare to be

00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:39.242
shut down.

00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:43.554
I don't play with a band,
I use a sound pedal which

00:01:43.554 --> 00:01:47.233
is all live and built up
so Wembley was always like

00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:49.980
even an arena I thought
I couldn't do an arena so

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:53.016
but then when I did an arena
and it was fine I was like,

00:01:53.016 --> 00:01:54.617
"Well we might as well try Wembley,

00:01:54.617 --> 00:01:56.259
"what's the worst that could happen?"

00:01:56.259 --> 00:01:57.996
The worst that could happen is you fail.

00:01:57.996 --> 00:02:01.711
But you still sold Wembley
so the worst that could

00:02:01.711 --> 00:02:03.637
happen is that you fail and
you move back to the arenas

00:02:03.637 --> 00:02:06.554
which is not, that's what
everyone else does so that's

00:02:06.554 --> 00:02:08.804
quite a cool thing.

00:02:08.804 --> 00:02:10.439
- [Charlie] How many people were there?

00:02:10.439 --> 00:02:12.403
- It was at 87,000 every night.

00:02:12.403 --> 00:02:14.459
- [Interviewer] 87,000.

00:02:14.459 --> 00:02:15.385
- Yeah.

00:02:15.385 --> 00:02:18.526
It's a big ol' stadium, but
the thing is like if you

00:02:18.526 --> 00:02:21.688
fail you've not lost
anything, and if you achieve

00:02:21.688 --> 00:02:25.925
and make it good the
possibilities are endless.

00:02:25.925 --> 00:02:29.380
- How imposing is social
media on your life?

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:30.499
Did you just get sick of it?

00:02:30.499 --> 00:02:31.873
You just wanted to detox?

00:02:31.873 --> 00:02:36.340
- No I enjoy diving into
it and kind of being on it

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:39.276
but it's just quite toxic
to be, you just end up

00:02:39.276 --> 00:02:41.247
getting really self
conscious about yourself.

00:02:41.247 --> 00:02:45.676
I wasn't a hugely self conscious
guy before and then you

00:02:45.676 --> 00:02:48.406
just read into everything
and you kind of end up

00:02:48.406 --> 00:02:51.197
searching for people
slagging you off and like

00:02:51.197 --> 00:02:53.813
looking for people saying mean things.

00:02:53.813 --> 00:02:54.646
Yeah.

00:02:54.646 --> 00:02:58.357
And since I've been off
it it's been glorious.

00:02:58.357 --> 00:03:00.349
I want a 30 year career
not a five year career.

00:03:00.349 --> 00:03:04.121
I don't want to do it, you know
we could have done stadiums

00:03:04.121 --> 00:03:06.090
a lot earlier on, we could
have done arenas a lot

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.375
earlier on, we could have
released five albums in five

00:03:08.375 --> 00:03:11.288
years but it's a marathon
and not a sprint.

00:03:11.288 --> 00:03:14.277
And I remember being around
a lot of my contemporaries

00:03:14.277 --> 00:03:15.993
that were doing so much
better than me and my manager

00:03:15.993 --> 00:03:17.785
being like, "Just slow
down, slow down, slow down."

00:03:17.785 --> 00:03:20.461
And now I'm still here six
years on and they're not and

00:03:20.461 --> 00:03:25.100
I think the whole music
industry you need to just

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:26.239
pace yourself.

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:28.139
Just take time to make great
albums, you don't have to

00:03:28.139 --> 00:03:31.666
stick out music all the
time to stay relevant.

00:03:31.666 --> 00:03:33.592
You'll stay relevant by making
great records and to make

00:03:33.592 --> 00:03:36.132
great records you have to
spend a lot of time to do them.

00:03:36.132 --> 00:03:38.753
I've only written songs from the heart.

00:03:38.753 --> 00:03:41.313
I don't really see the point
of making music if it's not

00:03:41.313 --> 00:03:43.961
an expression of self
or a form of therapy.

00:03:43.961 --> 00:03:48.212
If I have a bad day I want
to write a song, I don't

00:03:48.212 --> 00:03:50.370
want to write a song to have
a hit, I want to write a song

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.029
cause I wanted it to make me feel better.

00:03:52.029 --> 00:03:56.196
It's never been about the
audience or pleasing people

00:03:57.281 --> 00:03:59.616
or trying to fit in, it's
just been about myself and

00:03:59.616 --> 00:04:03.739
my love for music and I
guess that has come across.

00:04:03.739 --> 00:04:06.387
I think that this album is
very much, it's rooted in

00:04:06.387 --> 00:04:09.224
my comfort zone but there are
certain times where I step

00:04:09.224 --> 00:04:10.286
out of the comfort zone.

00:04:10.286 --> 00:04:13.660
And the reason I chose Sing
as the sort of lead single

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:15.710
for it, that being a step
out of the comfort zone

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:18.600
is I had an acoustic ballad
song I wanted to put out

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.773
first and whenever I played
it to people they were like,

00:04:20.773 --> 00:04:24.081
"Oh, it's nice, yeah, really
cool, really nice song."

00:04:24.081 --> 00:04:26.720
When you played Sing to people
it really polarized everyone.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.084
They either hated it or
they loved it but they spoke

00:04:29.084 --> 00:04:31.223
about it a lot, you know you'd
speak to people and they'd

00:04:31.223 --> 00:04:33.128
be like, "Oh, yeah I heard
about that song cause so and so

00:04:33.128 --> 00:04:33.961
"is talking about it."

00:04:33.961 --> 00:04:35.571
But no one was talking
about the acoustic tune.

00:04:35.571 --> 00:04:39.224
So I thought by sticking out
a song that sort of makes

00:04:39.224 --> 00:04:44.200
people talk it would be a
lot better for the album.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:48.367
I do work hard and I do try
and keep going but it could

00:04:49.482 --> 00:04:50.609
be a lot worse.

00:04:50.609 --> 00:04:53.241
This is the best job that
I could possibly have and I

00:04:53.241 --> 00:04:56.937
could be working in a
supermarket or something.

00:04:56.937 --> 00:05:00.687
The days off that don't
exist because I don't

00:05:02.325 --> 00:05:04.249
want them to exist.

00:05:04.249 --> 00:05:05.147
I want to keep having
stuff to do every day.

00:05:05.147 --> 00:05:07.165
It excites me to travel
around the world and play for

00:05:07.165 --> 00:05:09.915
people that genuinely love my
music and smile and they're

00:05:09.915 --> 00:05:12.859
all happy and people are nice
to me when we do interviews

00:05:12.859 --> 00:05:14.472
and stuff, it's good.

00:05:14.472 --> 00:05:16.330
I've made this album on
tour and I'm making the next

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:18.568
album on tour and it's
about finding the time, like

00:05:18.568 --> 00:05:21.123
I've got a producer to
bring a mobile studio into

00:05:21.123 --> 00:05:23.472
every dressing room that I go
into, and every hotel room,

00:05:23.472 --> 00:05:24.965
and on the tour bus, so.

00:05:24.965 --> 00:05:28.199
In between doing meet and
greets and the gig, you'll

00:05:28.199 --> 00:05:29.755
try and write a song and
then after the gig you'll

00:05:29.755 --> 00:05:31.103
try and write a song and
then on the bus journey

00:05:31.103 --> 00:05:32.615
to the next place you'll
try to write a song, so

00:05:32.615 --> 00:05:35.792
it's about finding the
time to make it happen.

00:05:35.792 --> 00:05:38.009
For me success is always
stepping out on stage and feeling

00:05:38.009 --> 00:05:40.991
people genuinely happy to hear your songs.

00:05:40.991 --> 00:05:45.229
Yeah, that's always been,
when I know a song's a hit

00:05:45.229 --> 00:05:47.790
is when you play it
and you just see people

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:48.983
light up in the crowd and smile.

00:05:48.983 --> 00:05:53.072
I think that's how you know
rather than seeing stats and

00:05:53.072 --> 00:05:53.905
figures.

00:05:53.905 --> 00:05:54.738
- Right.

00:05:54.738 --> 00:05:56.635
- Stats and figures are
great as well to look at.

00:05:56.635 --> 00:05:58.471
Everyone needs their ego
massaged sometimes but I think

00:05:58.471 --> 00:06:00.804
the gigs, gigs are the ones.

00:06:02.016 --> 00:06:03.773
- [Cameraman] What's happening Ed?

00:06:03.773 --> 00:06:06.106
- Just going to go sing a song.

00:06:10.267 --> 00:06:11.187
Should I just roll up?

00:06:11.187 --> 00:06:14.187
- [Cameraman] Just roll up, roll up.

00:06:16.494 --> 00:06:21.414
♫ I know you will still love me the same

00:06:21.414 --> 00:06:25.581
♫ Cause darling your
soul could never grow old

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:29.980
♫ It's evergreen

00:06:33.495 --> 00:06:35.596
♫ And baby your

00:06:35.596 --> 00:06:39.763
♫ Smile's forever in my mind and memory

00:06:44.037 --> 00:06:46.550
♫ I'm thinking about how

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:50.717
♫ People fall in love in mysterious ways

00:06:52.609 --> 00:06:56.776
♫ Maybe it's all part of the plan

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:03.522
♫ I'll just fall in love
with you every single

00:07:03.522 --> 00:07:07.689
♫ Day, I just want to tell you I am

00:07:09.689 --> 00:07:12.356
♫ So honey, now

00:07:14.977 --> 00:07:18.977
♫ Take me into your loving arms

00:07:21.084 --> 00:07:25.251
♫ Kiss me under the
light of a thousand stars

00:07:27.122 --> 00:07:31.324
♫ Place your head on my beating heart

00:07:31.324 --> 00:07:34.353
♫ I'm thinking out loud

00:07:34.353 --> 00:07:38.520
♫ But maybe we found
love right where we are

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:46.270
(applause)

00:07:50.055 --> 00:07:54.222
♫ La, la, la, la, la, la,
la, la, la, la, la, la, la

00:07:59.787 --> 00:08:00.903
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:08:00.903 --> 00:08:04.167
I made this video because
Husam Ezzi asked me to.

00:08:04.167 --> 00:08:05.427
If there's someone you'd
like to vote on for the

00:08:05.427 --> 00:08:07.774
next top 10 check out the
link in the description

00:08:07.774 --> 00:08:09.705
and go and have your say.

00:08:09.705 --> 00:08:11.280
Finally I want to give a
quick shout out to Jason

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:13.327
Bean, Jason thank you so
much for picking up a copy

00:08:13.327 --> 00:08:15.869
of my book, Your One Word,
and for posting a video

00:08:15.869 --> 00:08:18.077
about it on your YouTube
channel, I really appreciate

00:08:18.077 --> 00:08:21.092
the support man, and I'm so
glad you enjoyed the read.

00:08:21.092 --> 00:08:25.534
- [Jason] There it is, Your
One Word, by Evan Carmichael.

00:08:25.534 --> 00:08:28.042
- So thank you guys again for
watching, I believe in you.

00:08:28.042 --> 00:08:30.154
I hope you continue to believe
in yourself and whatever

00:08:30.154 --> 00:08:33.069
your one word is, much love.

00:08:33.069 --> 00:08:34.569
I'll see you soon.

00:08:40.716 --> 00:08:43.686
- Embrace your quirks,
embrace your weirdness and

00:08:43.686 --> 00:08:47.853
from a stuttering point of view
don't treat it as an issue.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:52.967
Work through it and get the
treatment that you want to get

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.239
but don't ever treat it as
an issue and don't see it as

00:08:56.239 --> 00:08:58.072
a plight on your life.

00:08:58.994 --> 00:09:01.536
The thing that makes you
cool is as you said earlier,

00:09:01.536 --> 00:09:02.369
not giving a.

00:09:02.369 --> 00:09:05.471
And I'm never going to hide
the fact that I'm a very

00:09:05.471 --> 00:09:07.230
competitive person and I
do want this to be biggest

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:10.272
album in the world and I'm
going to make sure it is

00:09:10.272 --> 00:09:11.384
the biggest album in the world.

00:09:11.384 --> 00:09:13.679
There's going to be no
stone left unturned.

00:09:13.679 --> 00:09:17.362
- I love that you have totally
stayed true to yourself

00:09:17.362 --> 00:09:21.021
in your songs, with your
style, just with your career

00:09:21.021 --> 00:09:21.854
in general.

00:09:21.854 --> 00:09:23.538
So if you could, I would
like to know how you would

00:09:23.538 --> 00:09:26.040
encourage other young twenty
somethings to stay true

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:29.256
to themselves without letting
other people bring them down.

00:09:29.256 --> 00:09:31.818
- No one can be you better than you can.

00:09:31.818 --> 00:09:34.406
I know that's quite an obvious
thing but I think people

00:09:34.406 --> 00:09:38.116
forget that people try to
model themselves on what other

00:09:38.116 --> 00:09:42.379
people are doing, and
everyone has their flaws

00:09:42.379 --> 00:09:45.212
and flaws are what make you human.

